# Power — configuration
#
# Copy this to .env and fill in what you need:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is git-ignored. This file is not, so never put a real secret in it.
#
# Nothing here is required to run Power locally. `pnpm dev` works with this
# file empty. Each section says what filling it in unlocks, and SETUP.md walks
# through where each value comes from, in the order worth doing them.
#
# Sections:
#   1  Running locally
#   2  Running somewhere other people can reach          <- required to be a connector
#   3  Storing data properly
#   4  Publishing apps to real hosting
#   5  Email
#   6  Services for the apps Power builds
#   7  Charging money
#   8  Tests


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1  RUNNING LOCALLY
#
# The defaults are already right. You only touch these to change a port or to
# put the data somewhere else.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Port the MCP server listens on.
PORT=4321

# Where projects, checkpoints and the local control plane live.
# POWER_LOCAL_ROOT=.power-local

# Local sign-in shortcuts. BOTH are refused off loopback, and dev auth declines
# any request that presented a credential, so a revoked token still fails.
# Leave them on for local work; never set them on a public host.
POWER_DEV_AUTH=1
POWER_DEV_LOGIN=1

# `local` writes a published release to disk and serves it, so publishing gives
# you a URL you can open with no hosting account. Anything else deploys for
# real. Chosen explicitly, never by falling back.
POWER_DEPLOYER=local


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2  RUNNING SOMEWHERE OTHER PEOPLE CAN REACH
#
# This is the section that turns Power from private into usable. Until these
# are set and the server is on public HTTPS, nobody can add it to Claude.
#
# POWER_MCP_URL must equal what a person types into Claude, byte for byte.
# A trailing slash or a missing path is the most common reason a connector
# fails with nothing useful in any log.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The public origin. Everything else is derived from it.
# POWER_ISSUER_URL=https://mcp.yourdomain.com

# The MCP endpoint itself. Defaults to <issuer>/mcp.
# POWER_MCP_URL=https://mcp.yourdomain.com/mcp

# Where previews are served. A preview is an application an assistant wrote,
# and it must not share an origin with the pages carrying the approve-plan and
# confirm-publish forms: same-origin script can read the CSRF token out of
# Power's own HTML, and no token or header check can substitute for a separate
# host. A subdomain pointing at this same server is enough.
#
# Unset, previews fall back to Power's own origin. That is fine on loopback and
# is warned about loudly anywhere else.
# POWER_PREVIEW_HOST=preview.yourdomain.com

# Browsers that may call /mcp. claude.ai and claude.com are always allowed.
# POWER_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# Host headers the server will answer to. Defaults to the issuer host plus
# loopback. A mismatch returns 421 rather than being quietly accepted.
# POWER_ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# Extra exact OAuth redirect URIs, comma separated. Claude's callback and
# loopback are always allowed, so this is only for a tool like MCP Inspector
# on a fixed address.
# POWER_EXTRA_REDIRECT_URIS=

# Token lifetimes, in seconds. The defaults are deliberate: a long-lived
# access token makes rotation and revocation decorative.
# POWER_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL=3600          # 1 hour
# POWER_REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL=2592000      # 30 days
# POWER_SESSION_TTL=2592000            # 30 days
# POWER_CODE_TTL=60                    # 1 minute
# POWER_LOGIN_TTL=900                  # 15 minutes

# Static bearer tokens for scripts, alongside OAuth. Format:
#   token:user_id:plan,token:user_id:plan        plan = free | pro | power
# POWER_TOKENS=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3  STORING DATA PROPERLY
#
# Locally, Power runs Postgres in-process and needs nothing here. In production
# both of these are required: without DATABASE_URL every node keeps its own
# OAuth tokens, and without POWER_SECRET_KEY the server refuses to start rather
# than pretend secrets are encrypted.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Control-plane Postgres. Neon, Supabase, RDS, anything that speaks Postgres.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/power

# 32 bytes, base64, used to encrypt stored credentials. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# Losing it means losing every stored credential. Keep a copy somewhere safe.
# POWER_SECRET_KEY=

# Moves per-user quota out of process memory. Needed the moment there are two
# nodes, or the limit is per-node and therefore not a limit.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4  PUBLISHING APPS TO REAL HOSTING
#
# Power operates no hosting. A published app goes to the account the person
# running it owns, so they keep it without you.
#
# Nothing goes here: the hosting token is collected from each user through a
# single-use browser form and stored encrypted per account. This section exists
# only to say so, because its absence looks like an omission.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Set POWER_DEPLOYER to anything other than `local` to deploy for real.
# POWER_DEPLOYER=vercel


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5  EMAIL
#
# Sign-in links are how people log in to Power. Without a key they are printed
# to the server log, which is fine locally and useless in production.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# https://resend.com/api-keys
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# The From address. Must be on a domain you have verified with the provider.
# POWER_MAIL_FROM=Power <login@yourdomain.com>


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6  SERVICES FOR THE APPS POWER BUILDS
#
# These are read by the SDK inside a generated app, not by Power itself. They
# belong in that app's own environment. They are listed here so the whole
# surface is in one place.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where a generated app keeps uploads. With no bucket set it uses local disk,
# which is fine until the app is deployed somewhere with an ephemeral
# filesystem; setting S3_BUCKET switches it to object storage. The driver
# speaks plain S3, so R2, MinIO and S3 itself all work.
# STORAGE_DIR=./data/storage

# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_REGION=                     # required on AWS; defaults to `auto` with a custom endpoint
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_ENDPOINT=                   # e.g. https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=           # defaults on with a custom endpoint, off on AWS

# Only for temporary credentials, which is what an instance role or an
# sts:AssumeRole hands back. AWS_SESSION_TOKEN is read too, under that name,
# so a role-based deployment works with the variables it is already given.
# S3_SESSION_TOKEN=

# Which environment an app is running in. Power sets this; do not set it by hand.
# POWER_ENV=workbench


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 7  CHARGING MONEY
#
# Power meters build actions today but bills nobody. Nothing reads this yet; it
# is here so the eventual shape is obvious.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# https://dashboard.stripe.com/apikeys
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 8  TESTS
#
# The isolation suite runs against PGlite always, and against a real Postgres
# too when this is set. Worth doing before trusting production: the policies
# are the whole security model.
#
# Point it at a THROWAWAY database. The suite creates roles and tables.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# POWER_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/power_test


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The marketing site, built separately from apps/site.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Canonical origin for the site's own links and metadata.
# SITE_URL=https://yourdomain.com
